  "profile": {
    "video_id": "iron-snail:_x-_PLprz3g",
    "purpose": "A wide tour of traditional wool sweater types (Norwegian, Icelandic, Shetland, Aran, cashmere, guide sweaters and the gansey). It covers the history and function behind each design, plus practical tips on fit, sleeve mobility, felting and finding high-quality vintage sweaters on eBay.",
    "video_type": "explainer",
    "thesis": "Most sweaters sold today copy a handful of traditional designs, each built for a specific function. Knowing that history and construction lets you pick the right sweater and find excellent ones cheaply, often used on eBay.",
    "key_takeaways": [
      "Norwegian lusekofte 'lice' patterns come from stranded knitting, which doubles the yarn layers for warmth; the same trick appears in Dale of Norway, Devold and L.L.Bean Norwegian sweaters.",
      "The Icelandic lopapeysa traditionally uses untwisted yarn (pencil roving) that blends the sheep's coarse guard wool with its soft insulating wool.",
      "Sleeve angle and taper decide mobility and how well a sweater layers: straight-out sleeves suit work, downward sleeves suit fashion.",
      "Shetland wool feels dry rather than soft, resists pilling and has a long knitting heritage; undyed Shetland blends natural sheep colours.",
      "The Aran sweater's fishermen-identification legends are largely folklore tied to an Irish economic and tourism push, which the creator sees as part of its real history.",
      "Felted eBay sweaters often show lost stitch definition or sizes that 'fit small'; they can sometimes be blocked back to size.",
      "Very cheap cashmere is often not genuine or pills quickly; buy used quality cashmere, or choose wool or lambswool instead.",
      "The guide sweaters covered are Peter Storm, vintage Patagonia, Andersen-Andersen, Dehen 1920 and Filson, with the Filson the one he wears most.",
      "Fully fashioned, handframed and handknit are good eBay search terms that signal care in construction.",
      "The gansey is presented as the most well-thought-out sweater: plain lower body for easy repair, reversible design, shorter sleeves and an underarm diamond gusset."
    ],
    "outline": [
      {
        "title": "Intro: sweater history, myths and video structure",
        "summary": "Opens with a Dale of Norway USA ski sweater and a gansey, Dutch gansey lore, and myths around fishermen's sweaters. Lays out the video's goal and its six-section structure.",
        "first_segment": 0,
        "last_segment": 4
      },
      {
        "title": "Norwegian sweater design",
        "summary": "Norway's national-pride sweater motifs and how the lusekofte 'lice' pattern uses stranded knitting to double the yarn layers for warmth, with Devold and L.L.Bean versions mentioned.",
        "first_segment": 4,
        "last_segment": 6
      },
      {
        "title": "Icelandic lopapeysa",
        "summary": "History of Icelandic sheep and the 1930s lopapeysa industry: seamless round yoke, stranded knitting, and untwisted yarn blending guard and insulating wool.",
        "first_segment": 6,
        "last_segment": 8
      },
      {
        "title": "Sponsor message: Bilt",
        "summary": "Sponsor read for Bilt rent rewards.",
        "first_segment": 8,
        "last_segment": 10
      },
      {
        "title": "Fit and mobility",
        "summary": "How sleeve angle (straight out vs. pointed down, raglan) and sleeve and body taper affect movement, layering, and rolling up sleeves for shorter wearers, using a Buck Mason example.",
        "first_segment": 10,
        "last_segment": 13
      },
      {
        "title": "Shetland wool",
        "summary": "Shetland sheep and the islands' knitting heritage, the dry, coarse but wearable hand of Shetland wool, and an undyed Shetland sweater with good texture and anti-pilling qualities.",
        "first_segment": 13,
        "last_segment": 16
      },
      {
        "title": "Aran sweaters",
        "summary": "Separates Aran sweater folklore (identifying drowned fishermen, symbolic stitches) from its likely origin as an Irish economic and tourism effort. Compares its loose, lofty white knit to the gansey.",
        "first_segment": 16,
        "last_segment": 21
      },
      {
        "title": "Sweater tip: felting",
        "summary": "How to spot a felted sweater on eBay (lost stitch definition, mislabelled sizes), blocking it back to size, and the upside of felted sweaters.",
        "first_segment": 21,
        "last_segment": 22
      },
      {
        "title": "Cashmere and alternatives",
        "summary": "Cashmere goat fibre, concerns about cheap cashmere that pills, a used William Lockie rub test, and a conversation with Maro of Wolf vs. Goat. Covers alternatives such as lambswool, alpaca, yak and possum, and why wool can beat cashmere at the same price.",
        "first_segment": 22,
        "last_segment": 26
      },
      {
        "title": "Guide sweaters",
        "summary": "Reviews guide-style sweaters: Peter Storm oiled wool, vintage Patagonia (cardigan stitch), Andersen-Andersen, Dehen 1920 quarter-zip and the $560 Filson guide sweater. Also shows the Iron Snail Billy Goat sample and explains fully fashioned construction.",
        "first_segment": 26,
        "last_segment": 37
      },
      {
        "title": "The Gansey",
        "summary": "A handknit gansey made by Jen: its 400-year design, plain lower body for repair, reversibility, shorter sleeves and underarm diamond gusset. Closes with thoughts on functional wool sweaters vs. fleece.",
        "first_segment": 37,
        "last_segment": 41
      }
    ],
